// Why `Readonly<>` and not just `Object.freeze`:
//   Object.freeze alone is a RUNTIME-only guard. `export const x: Record<string, T> = ...`
//   keeps the declared type `Record<string, T>`, so a future contributor writing
//   `toolRenderers.grep = myRenderer` still passes `bun check` and only blows up at
//   runtime, in someone else's session. Declaring the const `Readonly<...>` moves the
//   guard to compile time. VERIFIED: with the Readonly annotation in place,
//   `toolRenderers.probe_backdoor = toolRenderers.bash;` fails tsgo with
//   `TS2542: Index signature in type 'Readonly<Record<string, ToolRenderer>>' only permits reading.`
//   The runtime freeze stays because a cast (`as Record<string, ToolRenderer>`) still
//   gets past the type, and the type cannot stop a cast.
